{
  "headline": "White House says Melania Trump secured ceasefire in Russian-occupied Oleshky, Ukraine",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The White House has announced that U.S. First Lady Melania Trump has successfully secured a localized ceasefire between Russia and Ukraine in the southern Ukrainian city of Oleshky. According to the White House, the truce is scheduled to begin on 29 October, though neither Russia nor Ukraine has commented on the announcement. A senior Ukrainian official told Reuters that a ceasefire in the city would be established soon, while Russia has not provided a response. Neither party has confirmed the terms of the agreement. Oleshky, located in the Kherson region, was captured by Russian forces during the first week of the full-scale invasion in February 2022. While the city had a pre-war population of 24,000, an estimated 2,000 residents remain under Russian rule. The White House described the city as a front-line war city on the brink of disaster, noting that residents have faced limited electricity, mined roads, regular military attacks, and a lack of gas since 2023. Recent reports indicated that some remaining residents have resorted to eating garden weeds to survive due to food and water shortages."
    },
    {
      "text": "In a statement released by the White House, Melania Trump said she worked directly with both Russian and Ukrainian leadership to define the terms of the localized ceasefire. She stated that without her intervention, the possibility of bringing humanitarian aid into the city and evacuating the elderly would have been impossible. The plan involves non-military, civilian leaders from both countries working together to visit residents in their homes to determine required assistance. The First Lady noted that residents who wish to evacuate will receive coordinated support and transportation to a preferred destination, with the option to choose either Russia or Ukraine. This development follows previous efforts by the First Lady, who has focused on helping children in conflict and reportedly used backchannel communications with Vladimir Putin last year to assist in reuniting displaced or abducted children with their families."
    }
  ]
}
